# Who captures the regulator?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why did Stigler argue regulation often serves the regulated? How do concentrated interests beat diffuse ones?"
next: {"title":"Could a planned economy work?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/58-could-a-planned-economy-work.cast"}

## The dentists' board
sources:
    - id: minfed
      title: The rise of occupational licensing
      authors: Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis (on Kleiner's research)
      year: 2008
      finding: In the early 1950s less than 5% of the US workforce was covered by state licensing laws.
      url: https://minneapolisfed.org/article/2008/the-rise-of-occupational-licensing
    - id: bls2023
      title: Certification and licensing status of the employed by occupation, 2023 annual averages (CPS table 53)
      authors: US Bureau of Labor Statistics
      year: 2023
      finding: 21.6% of employed people held an active government-issued licence.
      url: https://www.bls.gov/cps/data/aa2023/cpsaat53.htm
    - id: scotus2015
      title: North Carolina State Board of Dental Examiners v. FTC, 574 U.S. 494
      authors: US Supreme Court
      year: 2015
      finding: Six of the board's eight members must be licensed, practising dentists elected by dentists; nondentists offered cheaper whitening by 2003; few complaints warned of harm, most were about low prices; the board sent at least 47 cease-and-desist letters and nondentists stopped offering whitening in North Carolina.
    - id: stigler1971
      title: The Theory of Economic Regulation
      authors: George J. Stigler
      year: 1971
      finding: '''As a rule, regulation is acquired by the industry and is designed and operated primarily for its benefit.'' Bell Journal of Economics and Management Science 2(1): 3–21.'

