    card title "The dentists' board"

Who really writes the rules for an industry: the public, or the industry itself? Start wi (@draw lic@)th how many people need the state's permission just to work.

B: In the early nineteen-fifties, fewer than one American worker in twenty needed a state licence for their job.
    highlight lic

My guess for today: still under one in ten. Doctors, lawyers, electricians, and not much else.

    ask "What share of US workers holds a licence today? Click the line at your guess." on lic store g default "10" voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}. It was 21.6% in 2023.
        wrong: You said {g}. It was 21.6% in 2023.

B: Twenty-one point six percent in 2023, about one worker in five. The surveys differ from the fifties, but the climb is real.
    draw onein5

    pause 0.3

    erase lic onein5

And who decides what a licensed job may do? Often a board of the lic (@draw board_box board_t@)ensed themselves. North Carolina's dental board is the famous case.

B: By law, six of its eight members are practising dentis (@draw elected@)ts, elected by the state's dentists. One hygienist, one consumer.
    draw d1 d2 d3 d4 d5 d6 hyg con seat_key

By 2003, kiosks and salons were whitening teeth, and chargi (@draw cheaper@)ng less than dentists did.
    draw kiosk

B: Dentists complained to the board. The Supreme Court later noted that few of the complaints warned of harm to patients. M (@draw price@)ost were about the low prices.
    draw dnode a_comp l_comp

The board sent at least forty-seven cease-and-desist letters, many warning that unlicensed dentistry is a crime. The kiosks stopped w (@draw strike stopped@)hitening teeth in North Carolina.
    draw a_let l_let

    pause 0.4

B: No bribe needed. The people writing the rule were the very sellers it protected.
    highlight dents effect box

    erase elected seat_key

None of this would have surprised George Stigler. In 1971 he wrote: a (@draw quote@)s a rule, regulation is acquired by the industry and is designed and operated primarily for its benefit.
    draw stigler

    erase quote stigler

B: That's r (@draw capture@)egulatory capture: the regulator ends up serving the industry it's meant to watch. Yet people with teeth far  (@highlight dnode@)outnumber dentists. Why do the few win?

## Three ways in
sources:
    - id: beim2009
      title: Report on Systemic Risk and Bank Supervision (discussion draft, 18 August 2009)
      authors: David O. Beim (for the Federal Reserve Bank of New York)
      year: 2009
      finding: Commissioned by NY Fed president William Dudley in 2009; found a supervisory culture of excessive deference to the banks and of examiners reluctant to speak up; released by the Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission in 2011.
      url: https://www.propublica.org/article/carmen-segarras-secret-recordings-from-inside-new-york-fed
    - id: dehaan2015
      title: 'The revolving door and the SEC''s enforcement outcomes: Initial evidence from civil litigation'
      authors: Ed deHaan, Simi Kedia, Kevin Koh, Shivaram Rajgopal
      year: 2015
      finding: 'Journal of Accounting and Economics 60(2): 65–96. SEC trial lawyers who later join defence firms are associated with more aggressive enforcement, consistent with a human-capital (signalling skill) motive, with some rent-seeking evidence in subsamples.'
    - id: kwak2014
      title: Cultural Capture and the Financial Crisis
      authors: James Kwak, in Carpenter & Moss (eds.), Preventing Regulatory Capture
      year: 2014
      finding: Regulators can be captured without any deal, by sharing the industry's identity, status and social networks (Cambridge University Press).

