    text ev2 "SEC lawyers:\ntougher, not softer" #2f6b8f cites ["dehaan2015"] x 500 y 262 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ev3 "Kwak:\ncultural capture" #2f6b8f cites ["kwak2014"] x 830 y 262 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Three ways in"

Four thousand d (@draw callback callback2@)ollars a dentist, two dollars a resident: the same arithmetic of who shows up. But a regulator can also be captured from the inside.

    erase callback callback2

B: From the  (@draw reg@)inside how? Nobody hands it a bribe.

First, informatio (@draw ch1 a1@)n. To write a rule about teeth or banks, you need facts, and the industry holds most of them.

B: So the regulator asks the industry what a rule would cost, and the industry answers. After 2008 the New York Fed asked a Columbia professor, David Beim, to study its own bank supervisors.
    highlight a1

He found supervisors too  (@draw ev1@)deferential to the banks, and examiners afraid to speak up.

    pause 0.3

B: Second, the revolvin (@draw ch2 a2@)g door. Today's examiner can be tomorrow's bank employee, at a far higher salary.

Surely that makes them soft. Yet a 2015 study of SEC lawyers found those heading for (@draw ev2@) private firms were tougher, not softer, as if showing future employers their skill.

B: Third, the same p (@draw ch3 a3@)eople. Regulators come from the industry, meet it every day, and come to see the world its way.

James Kwak calls t (@draw ev3@)hat cultural capture: no deal, just a shared view of what's reasonable.

    ask "Which channel did the North Carolina dentists need? Tap it." choose ["ch1","ch2","ch3"] answer ch3 voice b
        right: Same people: the board was the dentists themselves, so no lobbying or job offers were needed.
        wrong: Ask who sat on the board: here, same people.

Now put outsiders  (@fade a3 to 0.2@)on the board, and that arrow thins. Information and j (@highlight a1 a2@)obs still flow, so a fix has to reach them too.

B: So capture isn't one door but three, and none of them needs a villain.
    highlight reg effect box

    pause 0.5

## Captured for good?
sources:
    - id: kk2013
      title: Analyzing the Extent and Influence of Occupational Licensing on the Labor Market
      authors: Kleiner, M. M. & Krueger, A. B.
      year: 2013
      finding: Licensing is associated with about 18% higher wages (Journal of Labor Economics 31(S1)).
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w14979
    - id: ada1978
      title: Airline Deregulation Act of 1978
      authors: US Congress
      year: 1978
      finding: Phased out federal control of interstate airline fares and entry and wound up the Civil Aeronautics Board; the major carriers mostly opposed it; CAB chairman Alfred Kahn (1977–78) oversaw the deregulation of fares.
    - id: scotus2015
      title: North Carolina State Board of Dental Examiners v. FTC, 574 U.S. 494
      authors: US Supreme Court
      year: 2015
      finding: '6–3: a state board on which a controlling number of decisionmakers are active market participants must be actively supervised by the state to claim antitrust immunity.'
    - id: peltzman1976
      title: Toward a More General Theory of Regulation
      authors: Peltzman, S.
      year: 1976
      finding: Regulators trade off the support of producers and consumers rather than serving one group alone (Journal of Law and Economics 19(2)).
    - id: becker1983
      title: A Theory of Competition among Pressure Groups for Political Influence
      authors: Becker, G. S.
      year: 1983
      finding: Policies result from competition among pressure groups; growing deadweight losses strengthen the losing side (Quarterly Journal of Economics 98(3)).
    - id: carpenter2014
      title: 'Preventing Regulatory Capture: Special Interest Influence and How to Limit It'
      authors: Carpenter, D. & Moss, D. A. (eds.)
      year: 2014
      finding: Capture is often misdiagnosed and may be preventable and manageable (Cambridge University Press).
    - id: cooloff
      title: Post-employment restriction for senior examiners, 12 U.S.C. 1820(k)
      authors: US Congress (Intelligence Reform and Terrorism Prevention Act of 2004, s. 6303)
      year: 2004
      finding: A senior examiner of a bank may not take a job with that bank for one year after leaving the supervisor (implemented by the FDIC, OCC and Federal Reserve).
      url: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/12/part-264a

    text h_cap "Captured" #b5482e x 270 y 640 font_size 36 hidden true
    text h_not "Not destiny" #2f6b8f x 730 y 640 font_size 36 hidden true
    path divider #8f887c points [[500,660],[500,350]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    box c_pay "Licensed: ~18% higher pay" #b5482e cites ["kk2013"] x 270 y 565 width 420 height 80 radius 10 icon "money bag" font_size 30 hidden true
    box c_qual "Quality gain? unclear" #b5482e x 270 y 475 width 420 height 80 radius 10 icon "magnifying glass" font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_air "Airlines freed, 1978" #2f6b8f cites ["ada1978"] x 730 y 565 width 420 height 80 radius 10 icon airplane font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_court "Supreme Court 6–3, 2015" #2f6b8f cites ["scotus2015"] x 730 y 475 width 420 height 80 radius 10 icon "balance scale" font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_groups "Groups compete: Peltzman, Becker" #2f6b8f cites ["peltzman1976","becker1983"] x 730 y 385 width 420 height 80 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    box fix "Fixes: outsiders on boards · cooling-off periods · independent checks · sunset rules" #5f8a52 x 500 y 200 width 900 height 84 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    box st1 "Industries shape rules:\nmostly agreed" #3d3833 x 270 y 300 width 430 height 76 radius 10 font_size 27 hidden true
    box st2 "Capture as the rule:\ncontested" #b5482e cites ["carpenter2014"] x 730 y 300 width 430 height 76 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Captured for good?"

Stakes and channels explain the dent (@draw h_cap h_not divider@)ists. Do they explain every regulator? Stigler did say 'as a rule'.

B: Some of the ledger backs him. Lice (@draw c_pay@)nsed workers earn about eighteen percent more, by one careful estimate, and whether cu (@draw c_qual@)stomers get better quality for it is far less clear.

But look at the airlines. In 1978 Con (@draw n_air@)gress scrapped federal control of fares and routes, though most big carriers fought it.

B: And the regulator itself, the Civil Aeronautics Board under the economist Alfred Kahn, helped tear those rules down. Hardly a captured agency.
    highlight n_air

The dentists didn't keep their win either. In 2015 the (@draw n_court@) Supreme Court ruled six to three: a board run by active practitioners gets no antitrust shield without real state supervision.

B: Sam Peltzman and Gary Becker re (@draw n_groups@)fined Stigler's theory: regulators balance competing groups, so when the public's loss grows big enough, the rules can flip.

So how sure are we? That industrie (@draw st1@)s shape the rules written for them: mostly agreed.

B: That capture is (@draw st2@) the rule rather than a risk: contested. Daniel Carpenter and David Moss argue it is often misdiagnosed, and can be designed against.

Which points to the fixes: outsiders on the boards, c (@draw fix@)ooling-off periods before officials join the firms they watched, independent checks, and rules that expire unless renewed.

    erase fix

    quiz "Suppose a state's board of licensed beauticians writes the rules for hair braiding. Which rule does capture theory predict?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: The long course: it keeps cheaper rivals out of the board members' own market, whatever it does for hygiene.
        wrong: Ask which rule keeps cheaper rivals out of the board members' own market: here, the long beauty-school course.
        + Braiders must complete a long beauty-school course
        * Braiding needs no licence at all
        * A cheap one-day hygiene test

    quiz "A bank supervisor's examiners often leave to work for the banks they examined. Which rule aims straight at that channel?"
        right: The waiting period: it acts on the examiner's next job. US senior bank examiners face a one-year one.
        wrong: Pick the rule that acts on the examiner's next job, not on the banks: here, the waiting period.
        + A waiting period before an examiner may join a bank she examined
        * Higher capital requirements for banks
        * Hiring more examiners

    quiz "Which reform most directly weakens capture of a licensing board?" voice b
        right: Outside approval: it changes who decides, so the competitors no longer have the last word.
        wrong: Ask which one changes who decides: here, approval by officials who are not practitioners.
        + Officials who are not practitioners must approve its actions
        * Raise the board members' pay
        * Give the board more inspectors

B: Who captures the regulator? Whoever has the most at stake per head, until someone makes the many visible. A market failure is a reason to look at the state, never a guarantee the state will fix it.
    highlight h_cap h_not

