    '|b||Some of the ledger backs him. Licensed workers earn about eighteen percent more, by one careful estimate, and whether customers get better quality for it is far less clear.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11600
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1qsz8um'
    '|a||But look at the airlines. In 1978 Congress scrapped federal control of fares and routes, though most big carriers fought it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1a7v7q3'
    '|b||And the regulator itself, the Civil Aeronautics Board under the economist Alfred Kahn, helped tear those rules down. Hardly a captured agency.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:8xxbil'
    '|a||The dentists didn''t keep their win either. In 2015 the Supreme Court ruled six to three: a board run by active practitioners gets no antitrust shield without real state supervision.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:hqc6jc'
    '|b||Sam Peltzman and Gary Becker refined Stigler''s theory: regulators balance competing groups, so when the public''s loss grows big enough, the rules can flip.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:4gj1dr'
    '|a||So how sure are we? That industries shape the rules written for them: mostly agreed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:17wm5fs'
    '|b||That capture is the rule rather than a risk: contested. Daniel Carpenter and David Moss argue it is often misdiagnosed, and can be designed against.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1vq9j4a'
    '|a||Which points to the fixes: outsiders on the boards, cooling-off periods before officials join the firms they watched, independent checks, and rules that expire unless renewed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11200
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:zfpvpu'
    '|b||Three new cases. Suppose a state''s board of licensed beauticians writes the rules for hair braiding. Which rule does capture theory predict?':
